(Title) Tj
T*
(Near-infrared fluorescence improves lymph node detection compared to conventional dye in) Tj
T*
(breast cancer surgery) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Background: Accurate sentinel lymph node identification remains critical for breast cancer) Tj
T*
(staging. Conventional blue dye techniques have limitations including inconsistent) Tj
T*
(identification rates and occasional allergic reactions. We compared a novel fluorescent) Tj
T*
(imaging approach to standard dye tracing. Methods: 40 women with node-negative early-stage) Tj
T*
(breast cancer underwent sentinel lymph node biopsy using either near-infrared fluorescence) Tj
T*
(imaging \(n=20\) or patent blue V dye \(n=20\). All patients then received completion axillary) Tj
T*
(lymph node dissection for pathological correlation. Results: Near-infrared fluorescence) Tj
T*
(achieved 100% sentinel node identification \(20/20\) versus 75% with blue dye \(15/20,) Tj
T*
(p=0.047\). Median time to identification was shorter with fluorescence \(14.5 min vs 24 min,) Tj
T*
(p<0.001\) and more nodes were recovered per patient \(mean 3.6 vs 2.4, p=0.002\). Sensitivity) Tj
T*
(for detecting nodal metastases was 100% with fluorescence versus 75% with blue dye; false-) Tj
T*
(negative rate was 0% versus 25%, respectively. No fluorescence-related adverse events) Tj
T*
(occurred; blue dye caused dermal discoloration in 75% of patients and one allergic) Tj
T*
(reaction. Conclusions: Near-infrared fluorescence provides superior identification) Tj
T*
(accuracy and reduced complications compared to blue dye alone for sentinel lymph node) Tj
T*
(detection in early-stage breast cancer surgery.) Tj
ET
